data.content:
**How it works:**

- **`TimeProvider`** is built into .NET 8+. No packages, no custom interfaces.
- The **parameterless constructor** defaults to `TimeProvider.System` (real clock), so existing callers don't change.
- In tests, use `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` (a small NuGet) which gives you `FakeTimeProvider`:

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 12, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));

fake.SetUtcNow(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 11, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 12, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

Apply the same pattern to every class that reads `DateTimeOffset.UtcNow`: accept `TimeProvider` via constructor, default to `TimeProvider.System`.
